    // Edwin's story, one intro and thanks per chapter (chapters from data/story.ron).
    "story.edwin.arrives": "Edwin shuffles in, shaking imaginary rain off his coat.",
    "story.edwin.1.intro": "Evening, shopkeeper. It never rains here. Margaret and I used to sit on the porch whenever it rained … could you find me a little of that?",
    "story.edwin.1.thanks": "Ah. Wet stones and lilacs. Just like that porch. Keep my umbrella – I won't be needing it.",
    "story.edwin.2.intro": "Margaret made tea every morning. The tin always rattled before the kettle sang. I'd like to hear that rattle again.",
    "story.edwin.2.thanks": "Rattle, rattle … there she is in the kitchen. Here, her old cup. Mind the chip.",
    "story.edwin.3.intro": "It gets so cold between the nights. She used to knit for me, you know. Badly. I wore everything.",
    "story.edwin.3.thanks": "Warm. Crooked stitches would have been better, but it's warm. Take her blanket – it's warmer with someone using it.",
    "story.edwin.4.intro": "There was a tune at our wedding. I can almost remember it … something small that plays a melody?",
    "story.edwin.4.thanks": "That's the one. That's the one! I danced terribly. She laughed all evening. Here – our wedding photo.",
    "story.edwin.5.intro": "I never told her I was sorry for leaving first. Do you have paper? I think it's time I wrote to her.",
    "story.edwin.5.thanks": "Dear Margaret … yes. Yes, that will do. Hedda gave me this paper years ago – she said I'd need it one day. Thank you, shopkeeper. Keep her lantern lit for the next one who gets lost.",
    "story.edwin.ending": "Edwin folds the letter, tucks it into his coat and walks into the light. For a moment the whole shop smells of rain.",
    "story.plainly": "Let me say it plainly: I need {item}.",
    "order_slips.title": "Order slips",
    "order_slips.empty": "The next order a customer places will appear here.",
    "order_slips.line": "{who} · {item} ×{count} · due on night {night}",
    "order_slips.missing": "Awaiting stock",
    "order_slips.ready": "In stock",
    "order_slips.counter": "At the register",
    "order_slips.fulfilled": "Collected",
    "order_slips.expired": "Expired",
    "order_slips.pin": "Pin at the ferry",
    "order_slips.unpin": "Unpin",
    "order_slips.pinned": "Pinned order",
    "order.whisper": "I ordered something here last night.",
    "incident.fix_hint": "Click to fix it – until then the shop feels worse.",
    "incident.draught.name": "Draught",
    "incident.draught.start": "A draught whistles through the window and blows the candles out. Click the leaves on the window sill to close it.",
    "incident.draught.fixed": "You close the window latch. The candles burn steadily again.",
    "incident.crow.name": "Crow",
    "incident.crow.start": "A crow flutters in through the door and settles on the counter, cawing loudly.",
    "incident.crow.fixed": "You shoo the crow back out into the night.",
    "incident.leak.name": "Dripping roof",
    "incident.leak.start": "Drip, drip – the roof leaks and a cold puddle spreads across the floor.",
    "incident.leak.fixed": "You put a bucket under the drip and mop up the puddle.",
    "workbench.title": "Workbench",
    "workbench.rummage": "Search the old junk",
    "workbench.repair": "Repair {item} ({cost})",
    "workbench.hint": "Once an evening you can search the junk and repair one thing. Repaired things go into the storeroom like wares from the ferryman.",
    "workbench.done": "Done for today – the workbench waits for tomorrow evening.",
    "workbench.buy_hint": "With a workbench from the carpenter you could repair the broken things hidden in the old junk.",
    "workbench.broken_item": "{item} (broken)",
    "log.found_broken": "In the old junk you find: {item}.",
    "log.found_nothing": "You search every corner, but find nothing useful today.",
    "log.repaired": "Repaired at the workbench: {item}. It goes into the storeroom.",
    "decor.workbench.name": "Workbench",
    "story.ida.arrives": "The door opens on a gust of salt wind. Ida, the lighthouse keeper, stamps the sand off her boots.",
    "story.ida.1.intro": "So you're Hedda's heir. Good. My light has been dark since she left, and the lens is black with soot. Forty years I kept it clean … I need something soft to polish with.",
    "story.ida.1.thanks": "That's the stuff. Here – my logbook. Half the entries are in Hedda's hand; she kept me company on the long watches.",
    "story.ida.2.intro": "The lens is clean, but the clockwork that turns the light has stopped. It needs a good, steady heart – something that ticks.",
    "story.ida.2.thanks": "Tick, tick … she's turning again. Take the ship's bell. Hedda rang it every New Year's Eve, loud enough to wake the dead. It did, once.",
    "story.ida.3.intro": "The light turns, but it sweeps over empty water. The drowned ones drift out there and I can't tell where. I need something that always knows north.",
    "story.ida.3.thanks": "North … and there they are, every one of them. Here is my sea chart. Hedda marked the shop on it with a little ghost.",
    "story.ida.4.intro": "High water. They have gathered at the quay, but they won't come ashore without their ship. A small one would do – one that has never sunk.",
    "story.ida.4.thanks": "Look at them crowd round it! They remember now. Keep the lifebuoy – I won't need to throw it anymore.",
    "story.ida.5.intro": "The longest night. Hedda always carried something in her pockets that smelled of summer. I want the light to smell of her when it shines.",
    "story.ida.5.thanks": "There. Now she will find her way, too. Hedda left this with me for you – her apron. She said you'd grow into it.",
    "story.ida.1.log": "Lens cleaned of forty years of soot. The glass is clear again; Hedda's heir found a soft cloth.",
    "story.ida.2.log": "Clockwork wound and running. The light turns again, steady as a heartbeat.",
    "story.ida.3.log": "A compass set beside the lamp. The beam finds the drowned ones out on the water now, every one.",
    "story.ida.4.log": "High water. A little ship that never sank brought them ashore. Lifebuoy hung up for good.",
    "story.ida.5.log": "Lavender in the lamp room. The light smells of summer and of Hedda. Light fully restored.",
    "story.ida.ending": "Ida climbs the stairs one last time. Across the harbour the lighthouse blazes, and one by one the drowned sailors step into the ferryman's boat.",
    "story.lotte.arrives": "A little ghost girl in a woollen hat slips in and looks under every shelf. “Pfote? Pfote!”",
    "story.lotte.1.intro": "Have you seen my dog? He's called Pfote, he's small and fluffy and he loves chasing things that roll. If I had something he likes to chase, he might come out!",
    "story.lotte.1.thanks": "He'll smell the wool and come running, you'll see! … Not yet. But tomorrow, maybe.",
    "story.lotte.2.intro": "I heard scratching by the harbour last night. Pfote always went to the fish stalls. Do you have something that smells like fish?",
    "story.lotte.2.thanks": "Paw prints in the frost! He was here! I drew him for you, so you know what he looks like.",
    "story.lotte.3.intro": "He had something on his collar that rang when he ran. If he hears that sound again, he'll know I'm looking for him.",
    "story.lotte.3.thanks": "Ting-a-ling … listen! Something barked, far away. He's coming closer.",
    "story.lotte.4.intro": "I'm scared he'll run off again when I find him. Do you have something to hold him with? Not tight – just so we stay together.",
    "story.lotte.4.thanks": "There's a little shadow behind your counter, wagging! Pfote, come here … he's shy. One more night.",
    "story.lotte.5.intro": "He's so cold, he's shivering behind the stove. Do you have something warm and soft to wrap him in?",
    "story.lotte.5.thanks": "Pfote! There you are! … Can he stay here with you? It's warm in your shop. I'll visit him. Here's his basket.",
    "story.lotte.ending": "Pfote circles three times and curls up in his basket by the stove. Lotte waves from the door, smiling.",
    "story.anselm.arrives": "The door creaks open. An old man with a magnifying glass on his forehead counts his steps to the counter: tick, tock, tick.",
    "story.anselm.1.intro": "Anselm, clockmaker. The tower clock on the market stopped the night I died, and since then winter cannot find its way here. A little wheel is missing from the works.",
    "story.anselm.1.thanks": "That's the one. Listen … nothing yet. But the works will remember. I'll come back tomorrow, shopkeeper.",
    "story.anselm.2.intro": "The wheels are in place, but nobody has wound the clock for years. I lost my key somewhere between this world and the next.",
    "story.anselm.2.thanks": "Three turns, and the big hand moves a minute! Take this old cog – it kept the tower running for sixty years.",
    "story.anselm.3.intro": "The face is so dusty that nobody can read it. A clock nobody can read is just a wall. Something soft to make it shine, please.",
    "story.anselm.3.thanks": "There – the numbers gleam again in the moonlight. The village looks up. Did you see?",
    "story.anselm.4.intro": "The tower is going, but slow. I need a small, honest clock to set it by – one that keeps good time in a coat pocket.",
    "story.anselm.4.thanks": "Tick, tock – both in step at last. One more thing, and the season can turn.",
    "story.anselm.5.intro": "Midwinter. The clock will strike, but winter needs to know where to fall. Find me a little winter in a glass, and I'll show it the way.",
    "story.anselm.5.thanks": "Listen – twelve strokes. And look, the first flake! My cuckoo clock is yours; it never could keep quiet.",
    "story.anselm.ending": "The tower clock strikes midnight over the roofs. The first snow falls on the market, the harbour and your window, and Anselm walks into the light with his hat in his hand.",
    "season.2.epilogue": "The tower clock keeps time again, snow lies on the roofs, and Pfote sleeps by your stove. Winter has come to the village of spirits – and your shop is warm.",
    "story.fare_plainly": "Let me say it plainly: one coin from your drawer.",
    "story.mira.arrives": "The door swings open on a breath of wet earth. Mira, the gardener, wipes her muddy hands on her apron.",
    "story.mira.1.intro": "The snow is going, shopkeeper, but nothing grows – it has been winter here for so long. I need something to plant. Something small that sleeps until spring.",
    "story.mira.1.thanks": "Listen – the snow is dripping from the roofs. Spring heard us. I'll be back tomorrow.",
    "story.mira.2.intro": "The seeds are in the ground, but the ground is thirsty. I need something to carry water to them without drowning them.",
    "story.mira.2.thanks": "Look, the first green tips! Take this seedling – it wants a warm windowsill.",
    "story.mira.3.intro": "Bees won't come to a village that smells of cold stone. Something fragrant, please – something that smells of summer.",
    "story.mira.3.thanks": "Mmm. The first bee is already circling the market. Can you hear it?",
    "story.mira.4.intro": "Hedda kept a flower from every spring between the pages of her books. I'd like to start that again – one flower, kept forever.",
    "story.mira.4.thanks": "Hedda would have liked that. The windows on the square are full of flowers now.",
    "story.mira.5.intro": "One last thing: the gardens need a proper spring rain. Have you got a little of that, kept in a jar?",
    "story.mira.5.thanks": "Rain on new leaves – the best sound there is. This flower box is for your window. Water it now and then!",
    "story.mira.ending": "Mira walks out into the soft rain, and the whole market smells of earth and blossom.",
    "story.kasimir.arrives": "The ferryman ties up his boat and, for the first time, steps into your shop. He pushes back his hood: underneath is a tired old face.",
    "story.kasimir.1.intro": "I have carried every soul across this water. Lately the far shore drifts in the fog, and I lose my way. Something that always knows the way, if you please.",
    "story.kasimir.1.thanks": "North … and the far shore again. Thank you. There is something else I have lost – I will tell you tomorrow.",
    "story.kasimir.2.intro": "I have forgotten my own name. Nobody has said it for three hundred years. Some paper, please – if I write down everything I remember, perhaps it comes back.",
    "story.kasimir.2.thanks": "K … Ka … Kasimir. Kasimir! That's it. Here, my old boat board – I painted it when I was young.",
    "story.kasimir.3.intro": "Now that I know my name, I know what I want: to cross over myself, just once. But I need a light that no storm can blow out.",
    "story.kasimir.3.thanks": "A good flame. It will see me to the other side and back.",
    "story.kasimir.4.intro": "The tides on the far shore keep a different time. Something that ticks, so I am back before your first customer of the evening.",
    "story.kasimir.4.thanks": "Now I will be back in time. One thing is left, and it is the strangest of all.",
    "story.kasimir.5.intro": "Every soul pays the ferryman one coin. I have carried them all – and nobody ever carried me. Will you give me one coin for my own crossing?",
    "story.kasimir.5.thanks": "One coin. That's all it ever took. Keep my old pole, friend – I'll get a new one on the other side.",
    "story.kasimir.ending": "Kasimir rows out into the mist with his storm lantern burning. At dawn he is back at the quay, younger somehow, and he waves to you: “Evening, friend!”",
    "season.3.epilogue": "The gardens bloom, the river is calm, and the ferryman has a name again. Spring has come to the village of spirits – and your shop is its heart.",
    "finale.hedda.arrives": "The shop bell rings although the door stays shut. A small woman with lavender in her pocket floats in and smiles at the shelves as if she knew every one of them.",
    "finale.hedda.farewell": "So the ferryman did find you. Look at my old shop – fuller and warmer than I ever kept it. It's yours now, dear, properly. I'm going home with Ida. Leave a light on for the ones who come after.",
    "finale.hedda.scene": "Dawn over the harbour. Ida's light sweeps the water once more, out to where the ferry has gone. On the quay lies a letter in Hedda's hand.",
    "finale.hedda.letter": "My dear, by the time you read this, Ida and I will be out on the water. Her light is burning again – look how far it reaches. I kept this shop for forty years and never once saw it so full of whispering. Keep a lamp lit on the counter. Someone always comes back for it. – Hedda",
    "finale.continue": "Continue to the accounts  →",
    "season.1.epilogue": "The lighthouse shines over the water, the sailors have gone home, and Hedda sailed with them. The shop is yours – and the spirits know where to find it.",
    "letter.title": "A letter from Hedda",
    "letter.1": "Dear child, if you are reading this, the ferryman has found you. This little shop is yours now. It only opens between midnight and dawn, and its customers are no longer quite alive – don't be frightened, mostly they are lonely. Listen to what they whisper, count their coins carefully and never cheat them. Edwin will come by tonight; be kind to him. – Your great-aunt Hedda",
    "letter.2": "You found my old corner! When I was new here, I gave a poltergeist the wrong change and he rattled the shelves for a week. Mistakes happen. The spirits forgive more than the living do. – H.",
    "letter.3": "The back room. Ida and I drank tea here after her watches, back when we both still had a pulse. If she comes by, tell her I'm sorry the light went out. She'll know what I mean. – H.",
    "letter.4": "Now you have seen all of it. I didn't leave because I was tired, dear. Someone has to wait on the other shore for the ones the ferryman brings. When the longest night comes, keep the lighthouse burning, and I'll find my way. – Hedda",
    "log.parcel": "The ferryman leaves a parcel with {name}'s name on it. Hand it over at the counter.",
    "log.set_aside": "One {item} is set aside for {name} tonight, so no other ghost can take it.",
    "log.letter_found": "Behind the old junk you find a letter in Hedda's handwriting. You read it in the evening.",
    "evening.story_comes": "{name} comes tonight: {text}",
    "evening.parcel_waits": "The ferryman has left a parcel for {name}. It holds exactly what is needed.",
